N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): onsets of the whole clip folded onto the cycle that repeats best (1.14 s, one 2/4 bar at about 105 beats a minute, in eight sixteenths): on each beat, strong on the first sixteenth, weak on the second, strong on the third and fourth, which is the huayno cell (an eighth and two sixteenths). The pitch tracker (F4 to C6) found F the most-sung note, then B flat, C, D flat, G and A flat.
N:HARP BASS: fitting a fixed harmonic spectrum to every semitone from C2 to C4 in the sustained part, the harp's low strings alternate A flat 2 and E flat 3 an eighth apart (0.29 s) through most of the clip, root and fifth of A flat, with C4 the next strongest; so the harp holds A flat major while the singers' line centres on F, its relative minor, as huayno often does. The voices were tracked again on the sustained part alone, between B4 and F sharp 6: no phrase repeated clearly enough to write down.
N:UNCERTAIN: the melody (the tracker jumped between the voices and the harp, often an octave apart); whether the mode is F minor or B flat minor; where the bar begins.
